Start stream-copy trimmed MP4/MOV clips at their first shown frame (Zenvi)

Symptom (libopenshot v1.0.0; unchanged in v1.0.1 and develop 0fbacca9):
GetFrame(1) of a stream-copy trimmed MP4 (`ffmpeg -ss X -i in -c copy`, a
phone "trim" that does not re-encode) decodes the whole file, takes 18-75 s
on a long clip, and returns a black, silent frame.

Cause: a stream copy has to start at the keyframe before the cut, so the
file carries an edit list that hides the pre-roll between that keyframe and
the cut. FFmpeg's mov demuxer still returns the pre-roll packets (the decoder
needs them as references) but flags them AV_PKT_FLAG_DISCARD, and the decoder
never outputs a picture for them. UpdatePTSOffset() anchored the timestamp
origin on the first packet, discard flag or not, so frames 1..N mapped to
pictures that never arrive. In 1.0 the image fallbacks refuse a "future"
picture and CheckWorkingFrames() skips a frame without an image until EOF,
while ProcessVideoPacket() re-adds the requested frame on every packet. So
frame 1 waits for the end of the file and is then filled with black. The
whole clip also plays late by the pre-roll length, and its last frames fall
past video_length.

Fix:
1. UpdatePTSOffset() skips discard packets when it picks the timestamp
   origin. After a skipped video pre-roll it anchors on the stream's
   start_time, the first picture shown: with B-frames, the first kept packet
   in decode order can be a later picture. Frame 1 is then the first picture
   ffmpeg and QuickTime show. Audio and video still share one offset, so A/V
   sync is unchanged. Files without discard packets take the old path.
2. Safety net in CheckWorkingFrames(): when nothing has been finalized since
   the last open or seek, both streams are past a frame that has no image,
   and the first decoded picture comes later, that frame takes the earliest
   decoded picture instead of waiting for EOF. This covers leading gaps that
   carry no discard flag.

Behaviour change: such clips now start where QuickTime starts them. Under
libopenshot 0.5.0 they started late by the audio pre-roll (about 1 s for an
ffmpeg trim) behind a held first picture, so a project edited with 0.5.0
sees that clip's content about that much earlier.

Upstream: not fixed in v1.0.1 or develop 0fbacca9. Drop this patch when
moving to a libopenshot tag where GetFrame(1) of a stream-copy trim returns
the first shown picture quickly (installer/mac-patches/README.md).

